  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Robbie Ray held the line. Six innings, one run. And the Giants never trailed.",
    "what_this_means": "They left seven runners on base through the first four innings. That's the kind of game this is right now: pitching buys a window, the lineup shows up selectively, and the margin holds because nobody else in the division is executing better. Ray's 3.08 ERA gives them a chance every five days. The bats converted twice in twelve RISP opportunities and made it enough. Devers went 3-for-4 with an RBI. Eldridge added two hits. The bullpen closed it clean. Kilian, Smith, and Miller combining for three shutout innings with three strikeouts. But here's what matters: they led from the first inning on and never loosened their grip. This team doesn't need dominant offense. It needs disciplined pitching and just enough timely contact. They got both. Two wins in a row. The inconsistency hasn't disappeared. It's just paused.",
    "what_to_watch": "San Diego's Bradgley Rodriguez comes in tonight with a 2.33 ERA, but the Padres are batting .233 as a lineup. That's the opening Carson Whisenhunt needs.",
